Z||000038||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||11:16 AM||||antifreeze is alcohol and if you get it hot enough (spray it on an exhaust manifold for example) it will burn, the water vaporizes and the alcohol burns.<P>  I dont know the specific flash point though, I dont think you can light it with a match for example.||||reg||1
Z||000039||Galen||05-17-2001||02:13 PM||||I beg to differ, Antifreeze is not alcohol. 'Ethylene glycole' would be the correct answer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe Auto 4Banger - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR>AIM: WookieeNC||||reg||1
Z||000040||Pontiaddict||05-17-2001||02:58 PM||||ethylene = A colorless, flammable gas, C2H4, derived from natural gas and petroleum and used as a source of many organic compounds, in welding and cutting metals, to color citrus fruits, and as an anesthetic. Also called ethene.<P>glycol = Any of various alcohols containing two hydroxyl groups. <P>Yes, antifreeze is flammable. If you get it hot enough, plain water will burn too.<P>As for stupidest fiero BS, Plastic lugnuts, plastic fuel lines that melted and caused fires. "That whole car is made out of fiberglass or something, even the frame."<P>||||reg||1
Z||000041||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||03:13 PM||||water will burn too?!<P>water is hydrogen, that is already 'burned' (oxidized).<P>very hot water is steam - steam doenst burn.||||reg||1
Z||000042||JSocha||05-17-2001||03:49 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> &lt;SLAP&gt;  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>You better all tell your local fire department not to use water (H20) anymore as it could burn!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000043||Pontiaddict||05-17-2001||07:10 PM||||If you get water VERY HOT, way past the point of turning into steam, it will split back into oxygen and hydrogen and burn. If you dump water on a candle it won't do anything but put the candle out. If you try to put out a large magnesium fire (say, doesn't that happen daily?) with water, it will just make everything worse.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000044||FieroLisa||05-17-2001||07:26 PM||||This really doesn't have much to do with the topic, but did any of you guys see that cheesy made-for-tv movie "atomic train"? In one scene they found out if they poured water on the burning runaway train it would make it explode and burn up more.<P>I thought the movie was less cheesy when we had a runaway train go through Ohio with supposedly dangerous materials for 2 hours when the conductor, or whatever you call them, had a heart attack.||||reg||1
Z||000045||StuGood||05-17-2001||08:22 PM||||I remember "atomic train," from many years ago.  It was an extremely realistic movie  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>I must have been really bored.  I can hardly believe they actually aired that movie more than once! ||||reg||10
Z||000046||Oreif||05-17-2001||08:36 PM||||Magnesium fires cannot be put out with water. The water will not catch on fire from the magnesium. The reason Magnesium is bad when on fire is because it creates it's own oxygen as it burns.  U.S. Military aircraft use Magnesium wheels. In the case of a Navy aircraft, If the wheel catches fire, it is pushed over the side. Underwater flares and roadside flares are made from magnesium powder. The roadside flares work in the rain and do not set the road on fire when it's raining.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000047||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||12:12 AM||||This is not really a rumor - I read it in the newpaper a few years ago so Im pretty sure it really happened - but it is the worst Fiero story Ive ever heard.<P>  a guy was leaning against his fiero in his driveway, talking to a neighbor, and turned to go get something.<P> You know how low the antenna is on some fieros, and how some of them slant back at an angle? Well the antenna went up his nose, crammed in hard.<P>  when he pulled it out a little piece of his brain was stuck to the ball on the end of it.<BR> <BR>  the worlds first "Fiero Lobotimy!"<P>  the guy said it didnt really hurt all that much, and he hasnt suffered any adverse effects from the incident.<P>  Thats one of the reasons I keep the head from a RoadRunner Pez on the end on my antenna.||||reg||1
Z||000048||Sootah||05-23-2001||04:44 AM||||Nah, probably just a nasty booger, your brain is completely encased in bone. He would have REALLY had to have rammed his had down hard, plus even if he did the antenna would have just bent.<P>-------------<BR>There are very few personal problems that can't be solved with a suitable application of high explosives.||||reg||1
Z||000049||ThatRickGuy||05-23-2001||09:24 AM||||Water can burn, but, you have to excite the molicules enough to separate the hydrogen. I remember doing it in chemistry way back when in HS. basicly, we ran a current through a small cup of water, the current would make the hydrogen leave the oxygen, and you could light it on fire. it wasn't a big fire or anything, just a little flash if enough gas had built up.<P>as for the worst fiero rumor EVER! early 1988 when people were rumoring the end of the fiero line.||||reg||1
Z||000050||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||10:54 AM||||No the report in the paper said the guy was taken to the hospital by his neighbor - and there was bleeding to be stopped - and he took the 'tissue' sample with him, and it was a piece of his brain - the doctors confirmed it and did extensive testing on him to see what damage it had done.<P>  Now I wish i had saved the article from the paper.<P>  for the buring water thing, when you separate H gas from water, either using electrolysis or extreem heat, then you are buring H gas, not water. water is what the H and O gasses combine into after they have burned. so in a sense water is like an ash - its whats left after everything that can burn has been burned.||||reg||1
Z||000051||ggarsk||05-23-2001||03:31 PM||||The Fiero lobotimy story is true...it happened in Portland,Oregon. But that kind of thing could happens with any car.||||reg||6
Z||000052||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||03:48 PM||||on most cars the end of the antenna is not at nostril level, but they are on fieros, esp the fieros on which the antenna is swept back on the same angle as the windshield. I have seen many of them like that.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
